.Dd November 9, 2026
.Dt VMMANAGER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vmmanager
.Nd terminal interface for vmd virtual machines
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl \-version | \-man | \-install\-man
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a full-screen terminal program for creating and running virtual machines
with
.Xr vmd 8 .
It lists every VM that
.Xr vmd 8
knows about, with its live state from
.Xr vmctl 8 ,
and offers actions on the VMs that belong to the user running it.
.Pp
Each VM gets one folder,
.Pa ~/vmmanager/<name> ,
holding its disk and a small
.Xr vmmanager.toml 5
file.
The VM's configuration stays in
.Xr vm.conf 5 ,
which remains the source of truth for everything
.Xr vmd 8
understands.
.Nm
only runs on
.Ox .
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl \-version
Print the version and exit.
.It Fl \-man
Show this manual page with
.Ic man \-l
without installing it.
This works on any system.
.It Fl \-install\-man
Install this page and
.Xr vmmanager.toml 5
into
.Pa /usr/local/man
and rebuild the database with
.Xr makewhatis 8 .
Needs
.Xr doas 1 .
.El
.Pp
On first start, users who may use
.Xr doas 1
are asked once whether to install the man pages system-wide.
Answering no is remembered and the question is not asked again.
After an upgrade, users who did not decline are asked again.
.Sh KEYS
The main list accepts these keys.
Actions apply to the selected VM and are offered only when they make sense
for its state; the footer shows the ones available.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ic n
Create a VM.
Asks for name, memory (default 1G), CPUs (1), disk size (20G), an optional
install ISO and whether the VM gets Internet access (default on).
The name must start with a letter and contain only letters, digits,
.Sq \&. ,
.Sq _
and
.Sq \- .
.It Ic s
Start the VM in the background.
.It Ic r
Run: start the VM and attach its serial console.
.It Ic o
Connect: attach the console of a running VM.
.It Ic x
Stop the VM.
The first stop is graceful; if the VM stays up,
.Nm
offers to force it.
.It Ic e
Update memory, CPUs, ISO and the Internet setting of a stopped VM.
Any other line of the VM's block is kept as written and shown read-only.
.It Ic d
Delete a stopped VM.
The configuration is removed first.
The VM's folder, with its disk, is kept unless you agree to remove it.
Creating a VM whose name still has a folder is refused.
.It Ic m
Migrate a VM that was not created by
.Nm
(see
.Sx MIGRATING ) .
.It Ic ?
Show this manual page.
.It Ic q
Quit.
.El
.Pp
On a VM console, type
.Ql ~.
at the start of a line to leave it and return to
.Nm .
.Ql ~^D
is also accepted, as printed by
.Xr vmctl 8
console.
.Pp
VMs owned by other users are listed but have no actions.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/vmmanager/<name>/<name>.qcow2
The VM's disk, created with
.Ic vmctl create .
.It Pa ~/vmmanager/<name>/vmmanager.toml
Extras that
.Xr vm.conf 5
cannot hold.
See
.Xr vmmanager.toml 5 .
.It Pa ~/vmmanager/state.toml
Tool state, such as the installed man page version.
.It Pa /etc/vm.conf
Gets one added line,
.Li include "/etc/vmmanager/vmmanager.conf" ,
under a
.Li # added by vmmanager
comment.
Migrated blocks are commented out with the marker
.Li # moved to vmmanager YYYY-MM-DD:\ .
Nothing else is changed.
.It Pa /etc/vmmanager/vmmanager.conf
Regenerated: one include line per managed VM.
.It Pa /etc/vmmanager/<user>/<name>.conf
The
.Ic vm
block of one managed VM.
It always has
.Ic owner <user>
and
.Ic disable ,
so
.Xr vmd 8
never starts it by itself.
.It Pa /etc/vmmanager/pf.conf
The NAT rules, loaded as a
.Xr pf.conf 5
anchor.
.It Pa /etc/pf.conf
Gets a marked 3-line block, see
.Sx NETWORKING .
.It Pa /etc/sysctl.conf
Gets the line
.Li net.inet.ip.forwarding=1
when IP forwarding was off.
.It Pa /usr/local/man/man8/vmmanager.8 , /usr/local/man/man5/vmmanager.toml.5
The installed pages.
.El
.Pp
Files under
.Pa /etc/vmmanager
are
.Li root:wheel
mode 0644 and directories 0755.
Existing files keep their mode.
.Sh PRIVILEGES
Listing VMs needs access to the
.Xr vmd 8
socket, which wheel members have.
For other users, the administrator must set
.Ic socket owner
in
.Xr vm.conf 5 .
.Pp
Starting, stopping and attaching consoles on your own VMs needs no root.
Creating, updating, deleting and migrating VMs, setting up the network,
enabling
.Xr vmd 8
and installing the man pages use
.Xr doas 1 .
Each root step is announced before the prompt.
.Pp
Without the
.Ic persist
option in
.Xr doas.conf 5 ,
every root step asks for the password.
Recommended:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
permit persist :wheel
.Ed
.Pp
Every change to
.Pa /etc
is checked with
.Ic vmd \-n
(or
.Ic pfctl \-nf
for pf).
If the check fails, every changed file is restored exactly as it was and the
error text is shown.
If
.Xr vmd 8
cannot reload after a create, migrate or remove, the change is kept and you
are told to run
.Ic doas vmctl reload .
.Pp
If
.Ic vmd \-n
already fails before
.Nm
changes anything,
.Nm
refuses every write action and shows the error.
Hand-written configuration is never repaired.
.Sh NETWORKING
A VM has one network setting, Internet on or off.
On means the block has a
.Ic local interface :
.Xr vmd 8
gives the guest an address in 100.64.0.0/10 and runs DHCP.
Off means no interface at all.
Nothing else (switches, bridges, port forwarding) is managed.
.Pp
So the guests can reach the internet,
.Nm
sets up the host once, when the first VM with Internet is created or migrated.
It asks first and shows what it will do:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Writes
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/pf.conf :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
match out on egress inet from 100.64.0.0/10 to any nat-to (egress)
pass in quick inet proto { udp tcp } from 100.64.0.0/10 to any port domain rdr-to 127.0.0.1 port domain
.Ed
.It
Appends to
.Pa /etc/pf.conf
this marked block, then loads it with
.Ic pfctl \-f :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# added by vmmanager
anchor "vmmanager"
load anchor "vmmanager" from "/etc/vmmanager/pf.conf"
.Ed
.It
Turns on IP forwarding with
.Ic sysctl net.inet.ip.forwarding=1
and adds that line to
.Pa /etc/sysctl.conf .
.It
Enables and starts
.Xr unwind 8
if neither it nor unbound is running.
Guests ask the host for DNS and pf redirects the queries to the resolver on
127.0.0.1.
.El
.Pp
A missing
.Pa /etc/pf.conf
is never created; the setup is refused.
If loading the rules fails, both files are restored.
.Pp
The check at start-up needs no root.
It only tests that
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/pf.conf
exists, that IP forwarding is on, and that unwind or unbound is running.
If the pf rules were flushed by hand,
.Nm
does not notice until the next boot or until you run
.Ic doas pfctl \-f /etc/pf.conf .
.Pp
The anchor is at the end of
.Pa /etc/pf.conf ,
so with last-match and
.Ic quick
its rules take effect over earlier ones.
Hand-written NAT or
.Ic rdr-to
lines are left alone.
If you had your own NAT rules for 100.64.0.0/10, you can delete them from
.Pa /etc/pf.conf
and run
.Ic doas pfctl \-f /etc/pf.conf ;
the anchor now does the job.
.Pp
To undo everything:
delete the three marked lines from
.Pa /etc/pf.conf ,
run
.Ic doas rm /etc/vmmanager/pf.conf ,
remove
.Li net.inet.ip.forwarding=1
from
.Pa /etc/sysctl.conf ,
reload with
.Ic doas pfctl \-f /etc/pf.conf ,
and if you do not want the resolver,
.Ic doas rcctl disable unwind
and
.Ic doas rcctl stop unwind .
Then
.Ic doas sysctl net.inet.ip.forwarding=0 .
.Sh MIGRATING
A VM that is in
.Xr vm.conf 5
but was not created by
.Nm
is listed greyed out and has one action, migrate
.Pq Ic m .
It is never done automatically.
It is offered only when the block's
.Ic owner
is you, or there is no
.Ic owner
line; in that case
.Ic owner <user>
is added.
.Pp
The VM must be stopped.
.Nm
then:
.Bl -enum
.It
Moves the disks into
.Pa ~/vmmanager/<name>/
(a rename, which keeps owner and mode).
.It
Writes the block, copied as it was but with the new disk paths, to
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/<user>/<name>.conf
and adds it to the aggregate file.
.It
Adds the include line to
.Pa /etc/vm.conf
if missing, and comments out the old block there with the marker
.Li # moved to vmmanager YYYY-MM-DD: .
Nothing is deleted.
.It
Checks with
.Ic vmd \-n
and reloads.
On failure the disks are moved back and every file is restored.
.El
.Pp
Migration is refused, with the reason shown, when:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
the VM is running or paused;
.It
the block uses macros, is an instance of a template, or shares a line with
other text;
.It
the block is not in
.Pa /etc/vm.conf
(for example it is in another included file);
.It
the owner is another user, or the name is already managed;
.It
a disk is missing, is a qcow2 with a backing file, is on another filesystem
than your home, is in a folder you cannot write to, or two disks have the same
file name;
.It
a disk is not a regular file owned by you or root (see
.Sx SECURITY ) ;
.It
the folder
.Pa ~/vmmanager/<name>
already exists.
.El
.Pp
To migrate by hand:
.Bl -enum
.It
Stop the VM.
.It
Run
.Ic mkdir \-p ~/vmmanager/<name>
and move the disks there.
.It
As root, create
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/<user>/<name>.conf
with the vm block, the new disk paths and
.Ic owner <user> .
.It
Add
.Li include "/etc/vmmanager/<user>/<name>.conf"
to
.Pa /etc/vmmanager/vmmanager.conf ,
and
.Li include "/etc/vmmanager/vmmanager.conf"
to
.Pa /etc/vm.conf
if missing.
.It
Comment out the old block in
.Pa /etc/vm.conf .
.It
Run
.Ic doas vmd \-n ,
then
.Ic doas vmctl reload .
.El
.Sh SECURITY
.Xr vmd 8
opens disk and ISO files as root.
A symbolic link swapped in for a disk, pointing at a device such as
.Pa /dev/sd0c ,
would give the guest the host's disk.
Before it starts a VM,
.Nm
therefore checks every disk and ISO path in the VM's block: each must be a
regular file, not a symbolic link or device, owned by the VM's owner or root.
Otherwise the VM is not started and the files are listed.
.Pp
Only the owner of a VM may act on it.
Other users' VMs are shown read-only.
Since every owner already has
.Xr doas 1 ,
this is defence in depth, not a barrier against an administrator.
.Pp
A config file writable by an ordinary user would be read by
.Xr vmd 8
as root, so everything under
.Pa /etc/vmmanager
is written through
.Xr doas 1
and owned by root.
.Sh HEALTH CHECK
At start-up
.Nm
checks, without asking for root:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
that the
.Xr vmd 8
socket is usable;
.It
that
.Xr vmd 8
is running, and offers
.Ic rcctl enable
and
.Ic rcctl start
if not;
.It
that
.Pa /etc/firmware/vmm-bios
exists, and offers
.Xr fw_update 8
if not;
.It
that the network setup is complete, see
.Sx NETWORKING ;
.It
that
.Ic vmd \-n
passes.
If a VM folder was deleted by hand, the check names the broken VM and, for
its owner, offers to remove its configuration.
.El
.Sh UNINSTALL
.Bl -enum
.It
Remove the program:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pipx uninstall vmmanager
.Ed
.It
Remove the man pages:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
doas rm /usr/local/man/man8/vmmanager.8 /usr/local/man/man5/vmmanager.toml.5 && doas makewhatis /usr/local/man
.Ed
.It
If your VMs should stop being managed, remove the line
.Li include "/etc/vmmanager/vmmanager.conf"
and its
.Li # added by vmmanager
comment from
.Pa /etc/vm.conf ,
then
.Ic doas rm \-r /etc/vmmanager
and
.Ic doas vmctl reload .
VMs migrated earlier were commented out in
.Pa /etc/vm.conf
with the marker
.Li # moved to vmmanager ;
remove the marker to restore a block, and move its disks back if you wish.
Remove the three marked lines from
.Pa /etc/pf.conf
as described in
.Sx NETWORKING
before deleting
.Pa /etc/vmmanager ,
or
.Xr pf.conf 5
will fail to load.
.It
Your VMs' disks stay in
.Pa ~/vmmanager ,
which you may keep or delete.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr vmctl 8 ,
.Xr vmd 8 ,
.Xr vm.conf 5 ,
.Xr vmmanager.toml 5 ,
.Xr pf.conf 5 ,
.Xr doas.conf 5 ,
.Xr unwind 8
